#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221091 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "35a158c3-37b6-5123-ba90-1fd05218ce43" :question/math-academy-id "e-21271" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4$.*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}t + α_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4t\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(t) = e^{t}; r(4t) = e^{4t}⇒{tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{t}; 4tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{4t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{1}t + α_{0} &= (\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(e^{4t} - e^{t} + 4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= e^{t} \\\\\n- 3α_{1}t &= -3(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t \\\\\n&= e^{t} - e^{4t} \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}((4e^{4t} - 4e^{t}) + (4e^{t} - e^{4t})) \\\\\n&= e^{4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & 0 \\\\ e^{t} - e^{4t} & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "18064c11-63d3-599a-b504-7b589f46c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21272" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}$.*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7α_{1}t + α_{0} & 4α_{1}t \\\\ -5α_{1}t & - α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues or $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad \\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((3 + 2\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n(3α_{1}t + α_{0}) + 2α_{1}\\textrm{i}t &= e^{3t}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}e^{3t}\\sin (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{3α_{1}t + α_{0} = e^{3t}\\cos (2t); 2tα_{1} = e^{3t}\\sin (2t)⇒{α_{1} = \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t); α_{0} = e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t))\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t)), \\\\\n4α_{1}t &= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 2e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- 5α_{1}t &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- α_{1}t + α_{0} &= -\\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t) & 2\\sin (2t) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\sin (2t) & \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "40a6318a-a1c9-55f3-afdb-02b43891d7b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21273" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6$, and it has multiplicity two.*" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8α_{1}t + α_{0} & -2α_{1}t \\\\ 2α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe only eigenvalue of $At$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(6t) = e^{6t}; \\frac{d}{dλ}r(6t) = e^{6t}⇒{6tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{6t}; α_{1} = e^{6t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n$\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}$.\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n$6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t)$.\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 8e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 + 2t), \\\\\n- 2α_{1}t &= -2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(-2t), \\\\\n2α_{1}t &= 2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(2t), \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 - 2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2t & -2t \\\\ 2t & 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "c0213c76-94e8-5ab3-a260-8a78a205589a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21724" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrices $A$ and $e^{At}$ above, compute $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{At}) &= Ae^{At} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & te^{t} \\\\ 0 & e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & (t + 1)e^{t} \\\\ 0 & e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f1a154c5-434e-5383-ba15-655422aebb15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220076} {:answer-field/id #uuid "26b496f1-86a3-585d-8d51-7626b5ce0be7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220077}] :question/id #uuid "cdf86a0b-ce7f-5a36-9106-b67d70a6ba24" :question/math-academy-id "q-323878" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $1$.*\n\n| $e^{At} = \\frac{1}{5}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-7e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-the-matrix-exponent-for-a-matrix-with-distinct-complex-eigenvalues\"></a>"}]}
